- How to adapt to deletions or sliding windows?
- How to calibrate bias for very small m?
- How to combine with stale-data pruning for bursty traffic?

## Why This Is Asked

Assesses ability to design streaming window data structures and perform amortized analysis under batched inputs, a common real-world challenge.

## Key Concepts

- Sliding window with evictions
- Top-k maintenance with exact counts
- Lazy cleanup in a heap to bound update cost
- Amortized analysis in batch-input settings
- Trade-offs vs recomputing from scratch

## Code Example

```javascript
// Sketch of a TopKWindow with exact counts and lazy heap maintenance
class TopKWindow {
  constructor(K, W) {
    this.K = K; this.W = W;
    this.counts = new Map();
    this.window = [];
    // Minimal placeholder for a max-heap with push/peek/pop
    this.heap = new MaxHeap((a,b) => b.count - a.count);
  }
  insert(id) {
    this.window.push(id);
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) + 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c});
    if (this.window.length > this.W) this.evict();
  }
  evict() {
    const id = this.window.shift();
    const c = (this.counts.get(id) || 0) - 1;
    this.counts.set(id, c);
    this.heap.push({id, count: c}); // lazy
  }
  topK() {
    // prune stale entries
    const out = [];
    while (out.length < this.K) {
      const top = this.heap.peek();
      if (!top) break;
      const real = this.counts.get(top.id) || 0;
      if (top.count !== real) {
        this.heap.pop();
        continue;
      }
      out.push(top.id);
      this.heap.pop();
    }
    return out;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle ties in top-3?
- How does changing W or B affect space/time, and can you adapt to varying K?

## Why This Question Is Asked
Tests the ability to balance space, accuracy, and latency for streaming distinct counts under a sliding window constraint.

## Key Concepts
- Counting Bloom Filter with aging counters
- Sliding window semantics and estimation bias
- Memory-accuracy trade-offs: probabilistic versus exact data structures

## Code Example
```javascript
class SlidingWindowCBF {
  constructor(m, k, windowSize) {
    this.counters = new Array(m).fill(0);
    this.ringBuffer = new Array(windowSize);
    this.head = 0;
    this.k = k;
  }

  hash(id, j) {
    const h = (id * 31 + j * 17) % m;
    return Math.abs(h);
  }

  add(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]++;
    }
    this.ringBuffer[this.head] = id;
    this.head = (this.head + 1) % this.ringBuffer.length;
  }

  remove(id) {
    for (let j = 0; j < this.k; j++) {
      this.counters[this.hash(id, j)]--;
    }
  }

  estimateDistinct() {
    return this.counters.reduce((a, b) => a + b, 0) / this.k;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to exploit DAG structure for incremental shortest paths with monotone updates and selective recomputation.

## Key Concepts
- DAG topological order
- Monotone updates and lazy propagation
- Selective relaxation focused on targets
- Amortized analysis of edge relaxations

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode for batch decreases
function applyDecreases(batch) {
  const queue = [];
  for (const e of batch) {
    if (dist[e.v] > dist[e.u] + e.newW) {
      dist[e.v] = dist[e.u] + e.newW;
      queue.push(e.v);
    }
  }
  queue.sort((a,b)=>topo[a]-topo[b]);
  while (queue.length) {
    const x = queue.shift();
    for (const y of adj[x]) {
      const w = weight(x,y);
      if (dist[y] > dist[x] + w) {
        dist[y] = dist[x] + w;
        queue.push(y);
      }
    }
  }
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle dynamic target set changes efficiently?
- What edge cases degrade the amortized bound and how could you mitigate them?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests incremental counting, batch updates, and tight per-event amortized bounds, plus modulo-based pair counting with constant extra space.

## Key Concepts
- Residue class bookkeeping with O(1) per update
- Pair counting with modulo constraints
- Amortized vs worst-case trade-offs

## Code Example
```javascript
// insert/update logic
function apply(x, D, freq, total, op){
  const r = x % D;
  const idx = (D - r) % D;
  if(op==='insert'){ total += freq[idx]; freq[r]++; }
  else { freq[r]--; total -= freq[idx]; }
  return total;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How does performance change as D scales with n?
- Can you adapt this to allow multiple modulo targets simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design dynamic spatial indices under batched inserts and to reason about amortized bounds, practical thresholds, and rebuild strategies in proximity queries common in ML data pipelines or trading systems.

## Key Concepts
- Fixed-grid hashing and locality
- Amortized analysis with batched updates
- Rebuild/rehash trade-offs and density handling
- Space-time trade-offs vs brute force

## Code Example
```javascript
class GridIndex {
  constructor(D){ this.D=D; this.s=D/Math.sqrt(2); this.buckets=new Map(); }
  cell(coord){ return Math.floor(coord/this.s); }
  key(x,y){ const cx = this.cell(x); const cy = this.cell(y); return cx+','+cy; }
  insert(p){ const x=p[0], y=p[1];
    for(let i=-1;i<=1;i++) for(let j=-1;j<=1;j++){
      const k=(Math.floor(x/this.s)+i)+','+(Math.floor(y/this.s)+j);
      const bucket=this.buckets.get(k); if(bucket){ for(const q of bucket){ if(Math.hypot(x-q[0],y-q[1])<=this.D) return true; } }
    }
    const kk=Math.floor(x/this.s)+','+Math.floor(y/this.s);
    if(!this.buckets.has(kk)) this.buckets.set(kk, []);
    this.buckets.get(kk).push(p);
    return false;
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle deletions or moving points?
- What are worst-case scenarios if density spikes in a single bucket, and how would you mitigate?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming cardinality with bounded memory using probabilistic data structures; beginner-friendly but shows real trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- HyperLogLog for distinct count estimation
- Hashing, leading-zero counting, registers
- Time: O(1) per item, Memory: O(m)
- Error ~1.04/√m; choose m from m ≈ (1.04/ε)^2

## Code Example
```javascript
// Minimal HyperLogLog sketch (illustrative)
class HLL {
  constructor(p = 12) {
    this.m = 1 << p;
    this.reg = new Uint8Array(this.m);
  }
  add(item) {
    const h = hash64(item); // 64-bit int hash
    const p = Math.log2(this.m);
    const j = Number(h >>> (64 - p));
    const w = h & ((1n << BigInt(64 - p)) - 1n);
    const rank = 1 + clz64(w);
    if (this.reg[j] < rank) this.reg[j] = rank;
  }
  estimate() {
    // bias-corrected estimator placeholder
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you pick p for a given error? Explain m↔ε relation.
- What tests would you run to validate accuracy on synthetic streams?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical online data-structure thinking for a sliding-window distinct problem, common in real-time analytics pipelines used by data platforms at Snowflake, Salesforce, and Instacart.

## Key Concepts
- Sliding window maintenance
- Hash map with counts
- Eviction policy and amortized analysis

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo
let window = []; // deque of last W ids
let counts = new Map();
function add(id){
  window.push(id);
  counts.set(id, (counts.get(id) || 0) + 1);
  if (window.length > W){
    const old = window.shift();
    const c = counts.get(old) - 1;
    if (c === 0) counts.delete(old); else counts.set(old,c);
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt this for approximate distinct in extremely high-throughput streams?
- What happens if events arrive in irregular batch sizes or with possible duplicates within a batch?

## Why This Is Asked
Streaming frequency sketches are common in prod telemetry. This question probes knowledge of CMS, accuracy guarantees, batch processing, and amortized space-time trade-offs.

## Key Concepts
- Count-Min Sketch (CMS)
- ε, δ error bounds
- Update/query complexity for batches
- Space complexity O(wd)
- Trade-offs vs exact counting

## Code Example
```javascript
// CMS skeleton
const w = Math.ceil(Math.E / eps);
const d = Math.ceil(Math.log(1/delta));
const table = Array.from({length: d}, () => Array(w).fill(0));

function update(x) { for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]++; }

function query(x) { let m = Infinity; for (let i = 0; i < d; i++) m = Math.min(m, table[i][hash_i(i, x) % w]); return m; }
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would deletions or non-monotone streams affect CMS choices?
- How would you monitor actual error in production without degrading performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of streaming algorithms with bounded memory and error guarantees. Reservoir sampling is a fundamental technique that demonstrates trade-offs between memory, accuracy, and per-element computational cost.

## Key Concepts
- **Reservoir Sampling**: Maintains a uniform random sample of size S from an infinite stream
- **Unbiased Estimator**: (N/S) * sumS has expectation equal to the true sum
- **Relative Error Bound**: Using Chernoff bounds, P(|X - E[X]| > epsilon * E[X]) <= 2 * exp(-S * epsilon^2 / 3), giving S >= (3/epsilon^2) * ln(2/delta)
- **Time Complexity**: O(1) per element (constant-time random number generation and potential replacement)
- **Space Complexity**: O(S) for storing the reservoir

## Derivation
The probability that any given stream element ends up in the final reservoir is S/N. By linearity of expectation, E[sumS] = S/N * (sum of all N values), so E[(N/S) * sumS] = true sum — the estimator is unbiased. The variance of sumS scales as O(N * M^2 / S), and the relative standard deviation is O(sqrt(N/S)), giving the error bounds above.

## Code Example
```javascript
let S = 100;
let N = 0, sumS = 0;
let sample = [];

function process(value) {
  N++;
  if (sample.length < S) {
    sample.push(value);
    sumS += value;
  } else {
    let j = Math.floor(Math.random() * N);
    if (j < S) {
      sumS += value - sample[j];
      sample[j] = value;
    }
  }
}

function estimateSum() {
  return N === 0 ? 0 : (N / S) * sumS;
}
```

## Worked Example
M=100, B=10, S=50. Stream: [5, 12, 7, 23, 8, ...]. After first batch of 5 values (N=5): reservoir = [5,12,7,23,8], sumS = 55, estimate = (5/5)*55 = 55 (exact). After 100 values with sum=2340: reservoir has 50 values summing to ~1170, estimate = (100/50)*1170 = 2340 (close to true). With S=50 and epsilon=0.1, delta ≈ exp(-50*0.01/3) ≈ 0.85 — increase S to 400 for delta=0.01.
